data: {"delta":"`[] == false`evaluates to`true`\nHere's the step-by-step explanation according to JavaScript's abstract equality comparison algorithm:\n\n1. When comparing different types using `==`, JavaScript converts one or both operands to a common type before comparing them.\n\n2. For the comparison `[] == false`, the algorithm first tries to convert both operands to primitives using the`ToPrimitive`operation.\n\n3. For the array `[]`(empty array):`ToPrimitive`is called with the`hint`of`\"default\"`(since no specific hint is provided for equality comparison).\n   - The array's`valueOf()`method returns the array itself, so JavaScript then tries`toString()`   - The`toString()`method of an empty array returns an empty string`\"\"`   - So`[]`converts to`\"\"`\n4. For the boolean `false``ToPrimitive``hint``\"default\"`   - Booleans are already primitives, so no conversion is needed.\n   - However, the algorithm then tries to convert the boolean to a number because the other operand (after conversion) is a string.`false`converts to the number`0`\n5. Now we're comparing `\"\" == 0`   - The empty string`\"\"``0`when compared with a number.\n\n6. Finally, `0 == 0``true`\nSo the complete conversion chain is:`[]``\"\"``0``false``0``0 == 0``true`\n\nThis is why`[] == false``true`in JavaScript.","conversationId":"cmvyoyx6w00c6rmvn9c5gssok"}

